    confidence: 'medium',
    reviewedAt: '2026-09-21',
    claim: 'The official Constitution of the People’s Republic of China records a socialist state framework, Communist Party leadership, people’s congresses, the State Council, courts, ownership forms, citizen rights and duties, and provisions concerning nationalities; it is primary evidence of formal design and state self-description, not proof of implementation or public consent. The 2022 Party Constitution records the Party’s Marxist-Leninist and socialist-with-Chinese-characteristics program and organizational leadership claims, while the 2024 government work report supplies a dated policy and performance self-description rather than an independent social or economic assessment. Saich provides a broad history of the Party’s revolutionary rise, consolidation, Mao-era disasters, post-Mao reform and adaptive survival; Walder offers a distinct account of the Maoist state from 1949 to 1976; Perry’s Anyuan study shows how local labor mobilization, cultural positioning and contested revolutionary memory complicate a purely top-down account; Naughton traces the economy from socialist legacies through reform, market coordination, state direction, labor, trade and environmental pressures; and Cabestan’s French-language institutional study analyzes the Party, state, army, cadre pathways and party-state–society relations. Across six axes, the evidence supports a changing and internally contested order: Mao-era collectivization and planning shifted toward market coordination, public ownership, state direction, private enterprise and developmental policy alongside inequality and environmental strain; revolutionary equality and social provision interacted with class, gender, ethnic, regional, rural–urban, migration, religious and dissent-related inequalities; Communist Party primacy, state and military authority, people’s congresses, courts, local cadres, administrative reform and periods of centralization structured authority without a competitive multiparty order; revolutionary and anti-imperial sovereignty claims and national rejuvenation interacted with ethnic, regional, class, gender, diaspora, Hong Kong, Macao and Taiwan histories; Soviet alliance, Cold War conflict, reform-era global integration, United States rivalry and wider international interdependence changed over time rather than forming one fixed foreign orientation; and Marxist secular state language, regulation of religious institutions, local and inherited religious traditions and revival existed without a clerical state, while the religious history remains under-researched here. These are dated records and source-attributed interpretations, not numerical scores or claims that China, the Party, the state, any ethnic or religious group, class, region, leader or government represents one ideology.',
    limitation: 'A bounded political history of the People’s Republic from 1949 through the official 2024 government-work-report endpoint, not a current-country claim or a score for Chinese people, ethnic groups, provinces, regions, classes, genders, religions, workers, peasants, entrepreneurs, dissidents, parties, the military, the state, Hong Kong, Macao, Taiwan or any post-2024 government. The official records establish institutional and normative self-descriptions; the books represent different scales, methods, periods, languages and standpoints. None proves uniform rights implementation, political consent, social outcomes, economic performance, public opinion, local practice, ethnic policy, religious freedom, judicial independence, military control or every community’s experience. Chinese-language scholarship and archives, local implementation, rural and urban variation, labor and women’s histories, Xinjiang and Tibet, Hong Kong and Macao, Taiwan relations, environmental politics, migration, religion, dissent, state-owned and private enterprise, and developments after 2024 remain open research tasks; no live 2026 status is asserted.',
    sourceIds: ['chinaConstitution2018Official', 'chinaCpcConstitution2022Official', 'chinaGovernmentWorkReport2024Official', 'chinaSaichRebelRuler2021', 'chinaWalderUnderMao2015', 'chinaPerryAnyuan2012', 'chinaNaughtonEconomy2018', 'chinaCabestanSystem2014'],
