    confidence: 'medium',
    reviewedAt: '2026-09-21',
    claim: 'The 1993 Constitution is primary evidence of the Russian Federation’s formal design: federal statehood, rights and freedoms, a directly elected presidency, Federal Assembly, government, courts, local self-government and amendment procedures. The 2020 constitutional-amendment law is primary evidence of a later normative and institutional refounding, while the official 2024 election record supplies a dated state-announced endpoint rather than independent proof of competition, equal political voice, voter experience or public consent. Colton offers a broad historical and political overview linking imperial and Soviet inheritances to post-Soviet democratization, Yeltsin and Putin; Gill provides a distinct, theoretically informed account of how elites and state-building structured an authoritarian polity rather than a simple Yeltsin-democrat/Putin-autocrat sequence; Stoner analyzes domestic regime determinants, economic and demographic capacity and the geographic reach of Russian state power; and Sperling foregrounds gender norms, sexualization, homophobia, patriotism, conscription and feminist activism as tools and sites of political legitimacy. Across six axes, the evidence supports a changing and internally contested order: privatization, oligarchic and state power, energy and resource dependence, market transition, sanctions and selective social provision interacted without one settled doctrine; formal rights and citizenship coexisted with class, regional, ethnic, gender, LGBTQ+, migration, labor, rural–urban and political-exclusion inequalities; semi-presidential constitutional design, federal institutions, courts, elections, security services, executive dominance, elite stabilization and centralization produced an authoritarian polity without erasing local variation or contestation; civic, Russian, regional, imperial and post-Soviet identities interacted with Indigenous, ethnic, migrant, religious, class, gender and diaspora histories without one voice representing all; post-Soviet sovereignty, European and United States rivalry, regional influence, security and military power changed across the period and cannot be reduced to one fixed foreign orientation; and Orthodox revival, traditional-family rhetoric, Muslim and other religious communities, secular institutions and feminist or LGBTQ+ activism existed within a formally secular constitutional order rather than a clerical state. These are dated records and source-attributed interpretations, not numerical scores or claims that Russia, Russians, Putin, the state, a region, ethnic group, religion, class, gender, party, military or government represents one ideology.',
    limitation: 'A bounded Russian Federation political history from the 1991 Soviet dissolution through the official 2024 presidential-election endpoint, not a current-country claim or a score for Russians, ethnic, Indigenous, regional, religious, gender, LGBTQ+, class, migrant, urban, rural, opposition, party, military, security, diaspora or post-2024 governmental communities. The constitutional and amendment records establish formal and normative design; the election record establishes a state-announced institutional endpoint; and the books differ in period, method, scale and standpoint. None proves uniform rights implementation, political consent, electoral fairness, judicial independence, federal practice, economic performance, social outcomes, civilian control, foreign-policy motive or every locality’s experience. Russian-language scholarship and archives, regional and Indigenous histories, North Caucasus, Siberia and Far East, Ukraine and occupied-territory records, oligarchic and labor histories, women’s, LGBTQ+, religious, migrant and diaspora research, independent election observation, 2024 aftermath and developments after 2024 remain open research tasks; no live 2026 status is asserted.',
