    claim: 'The 2008 Constitution establishes Ecuador’s formal rights, plurinational, intercultural, secular and presidential framework, while the 2023 CNE record anchors the early election that brought Noboa to the interim presidency. Executive Decree 111 records the government’s January 2024 addition of an internal-armed-conflict basis to the state of exception and the mobilization of security institutions; it is a primary executive self-description, not an independent legal or human-rights finding. The CNE’s 2024 results record an eleven-question referendum and popular consultation with mixed recorded outcomes across security, constitutional, justice and economic questions, while the CNE’s 2025 record fixes the presidential second-round result, adjudication and May transfer of office. The CNE’s November 2025 referendum and popular-consultation record provides a further constitutional-contestation endpoint. Freidenberg and Pachano supply a systematic Spanish-language account of institutions, executives, legislatures, parties, electoral systems and Indigenous and regional actors; Pachano’s later comparative study supplies a distinct democratic-quality and left-turn lens; Becker reconstructs Indigenous and Indigenous-leftist movement history; de la Torre supplies a focused populism and political-culture account; and Blanksten’s historical constitutional study adds a longer institutional perspective while predating the selected period. Across six axes, the bounded evidence supports a mixed and contested reading: dollarization, fiscal pressure, security spending, private markets, extractive dependence, labor and social-policy disputes rather than a single economic doctrine; constitutional rights and plural identities alongside violence, insecurity, emergency power and unequal implementation; executive, military, police, legislative, judicial, electoral, party and movement authority rather than one stable center; civic Ecuadorian identity interacting with Indigenous, regional, class, gender, migration, party and Correísta–anti-Correísta identities; external security, financial, regional and diplomatic relationships shaping but not defining the period’s foreign orientation; and Catholic historical influence within a formally secular and religiously plural order. These are dated institutional records and source-attributed interpretations, not numerical scores or claims that Ecuador, Noboa, ADN, Correísmo, any voter, region, party, security institution, Indigenous nation or later government represents one ideology.',
