    claim: 'Law 11/2019 anchors the Maputo Peace and National Reconciliation Agreement in Mozambique’s domestic legal record, but does not prove completed disarmament, reconciliation or equal implementation. The Constitutional Council’s 48/CC/2023 record validates the 11 October municipal elections and records re-verification and changed results in selected municipalities, establishing a formal judicial and electoral process rather than independent proof of fairness or uniform local experience. The Council’s 24/CC/2024 proclamation record fixes the formal result of the 9 October presidential, legislative, provincial-assembly and governor elections. The EU Election Observation Mission, whose final report was presented after nationwide observation, identified flaws in voter registration, counting, tabulation, transparency and electoral management; its presentation record also attributed more than 300 post-election political-violence deaths to that period, a mission statement rather than a universally settled casualty count. Manning’s book-length study supplies the post-1992 democratization and elite-bargaining context; Morier-Genoud’s socio-historical monograph interprets Muslim politics, state mediation and the origins of the Cabo Delgado insurgency; and Svicevic and Bradley’s edited volume supplies a distinct international-humanitarian-law and regional-security lens on that conflict. On six axes, the bounded evidence supports a mixed and contested reading: economically, peace, security, displacement, extractive and development pressures interact without establishing one doctrine or uniform outcomes; socially, formal rights and electoral participation coexist with insurgency, displacement, regional, religious, language and local inequalities that no source treats as one national experience; on authority, executive and party dominance, RENAMO–FRELIMO bargaining, courts, electoral bodies, municipal and provincial institutions, security forces, religious actors and external missions interact rather than forming one stable center; on identity, Mozambican sovereignty and national reconciliation intersect with liberation-party memory, opposition histories, provincial and religious belonging without one identity speaking for all Mozambicans; external orientation is partially documented through regional security responses, international observation and humanitarian-law debates, but this is not a complete foreign-policy history; and Muslim, Christian, customary and secular constitutional traditions matter within a formally non-clerical state rather than establishing a single religious order. These are dated institutional records and source-attributed scholarly interpretations, not numerical scores or claims that Mozambique, FRELIMO, RENAMO, Cabo Delgado, any religion, region, party, voter, observer or later government represents one ideology.',
