GEOGRAPHY_CASES.push(caseRecord({
  id: 'south-african-2024-government-national-unity-coalition-transition',
  labelId: 'south-african-2024-government-national-unity-coalition-transition',
  placeId: 'south-africa',
  relationship: 'implemented',
  actor: 'The ANC-led national executive; the parties to the 2024 Government of National Unity, including the Democratic Alliance; the President, Cabinet, National Assembly, party leaders and the GNU coordination mechanisms described in dated official records',
  historicalSetting: 'South Africa’s post-29 May 2024 national election, June political compact and subsequent national executive/parliamentary coordination through the DA leader’s public statement of 22 September 2026',
  startYear: 2024,
  endYear: 2026,
  periodLabel: '2024–2026 · election-driven coalition formation, negotiated governing programme and contested inter-party coordination',
  datePrecision: 'year',
  evidenceKind: 'official election record and primary political compact, dated executive and parliamentary statements, two independent book-length election/coalition perspectives and an academic review',
  confidence: 'medium',
  reviewedAt: '2026-09-23',
  claim: 'The Electoral Commission’s 2024 results record the electoral setting for the coalition transition: the ANC won the largest share but lost its national parliamentary majority, after which parties negotiated a coalition governing arrangement. The parties’ 14 June Statement of Intent describes the GNU as voluntary and sets out shared commitments and decision procedures, including consensus and “sufficient consensus”; it records a negotiated political compact, not proof that parties agreed on every issue or delivered the stated programme. In July 2024 the President publicly described a ten-party arrangement and its proposed programme. Those executive and party texts are primary evidence of institutional and political self-description.\n\nThe shared economic language is deliberately mixed: the June compact pairs faster inclusive growth, investment and fiscal sustainability with redistribution, land reform, work, social protection and workers’ rights. The same document’s commitments to constitutionalism, non-racialism, non-sexism, social justice and state capacity are common programme language, not an independently verified account of implementation. The negotiated format is significant for the authority axis: it operates through a constitutional presidency, Cabinet and Parliament while official records describe additional inter-party coordination, including a Clearing House mechanism. These records establish documented procedures and disputes, not how much influence each party exercised in private or whether the arrangements consistently worked.\n\nThe six-axis lens therefore remains issue-specific rather than a single score or stable ideology. Economically, growth-and-investment priorities coexist in the compact with redistribution and social protection, while the formation-era scholarship treats coalition bargaining and ideological distance as open questions. Socially, equality commitments are formal shared positions; the reviewed record does not establish lived equality or uniform coalition policy. On authority, constitutional institutions and negotiated party coordination both matter, but the available sources do not show the full internal distribution of power. On identity, the compact invokes unity in diversity and national dialogue; this cannot be treated as representing all voters or communities. On foreign affairs, the compact’s stated principles document a policy frame, but no reviewed independent evidence here tests later practice. Religion is not sufficiently evidenced for a meaningful classification and is left unassessed. No six-axis scores are assigned.\n\nThe literature supplies two independent book-length formation-era perspectives: Susan Booysen’s MISTRA edited research volume examines the election, negotiations, cabinet formation and early coalition tests; the Schulz-Herzenberg and Southall edited volume provides a separate account of the election and path to coalition. Stephen Chan’s 2025 scholarly review explicitly identifies a need for subsequent mid-term assessment. These works illuminate the formation period but do not constitute a book-length retrospective covering the whole 2024–September 2026 interval. Later primary records show that leaders acknowledged budget-process difficulties in November 2025, the Deputy President described political differences and a coordination mechanism in Parliament, the President issued executive changes after DA consultation in June 2026, and DA leader Geordin Hill-Lewis publicly defended continued participation on 22 September 2026. The last item is partisan self-description, not neutral evidence of coalition success.\n\nThis episode should not be conflated with the 1994 Government of National Unity formed during the negotiated transition from apartheid: the MISTRA volume raises historical comparison as a question, while the evidence reviewed here does not support treating the two arrangements as equivalent in origin or purpose. Related ideological labels are illustrative partial comparisons only. The record supports a bounded account of coalition formation, stated commitments, institutional channels and publicly documented disagreement; it does not establish voter intent, overall GNU effectiveness, policy delivery, distributional outcomes, party members’ private views or a single ideological position shared by the country.',
  limitation: 'This is one bounded national-level political episode, not a present-day ideological score for South Africa, all voters, any racial, linguistic, religious, class or regional community, or the full history of coalition politics. The source set is strongest on formal election results, published coalition commitments and dated executive/party self-descriptions. The two book-length perspectives are contemporaneous with coalition formation; the reviewed academic critique notes the need for later mid-term work, and no book-length study of the complete period through 22 September 2026 was located in this review. Official statements are interested accounts, while the DA item is partisan advocacy. The evidence does not independently establish agreement among parties, private bargaining or relative power, coalition performance, policy enactment, social outcomes, public consent, local/provincial arrangements or the experiences of communities outside national elite records. The 1994 GNU is not treated as equivalent. No six-axis scores are warranted; religion remains unassessed. Further work should examine later coalition agreements and meeting records, parliamentary and budget implementation, provincial/local cases, independent social/economic outcome data, party and civil-society archives, and multilingual scholarship. Only original paraphrase and bibliographic metadata are recorded; no protected book or statement text is republished.',
  sourceIds: ['southAfricaIec2024NationalElection', 'southAfricaConstitution1996Rights', 'southAfricaGnuStatementIntent2024', 'southAfricaOpeningParliament2024', 'southAfricaMistraElectionCoalitions2024', 'southAfricaSchulzHerzenbergSouthallElection2024', 'southAfricaElectionBookReviewChan2025', 'southAfricaGnuLeadersRetreat2025', 'southAfricaDeputyPresidentGnuAnswers2025', 'southAfricaGnuStateOfNation2026', 'southAfricaGnuExecutiveChanges2026', 'southAfricaDaGnuPosition2026'],
  locator: 'Electoral Commission, 2024 National and Provincial Elections Report; Constitution of the Republic of South Africa, 1996; GNU parties’ Statement of Intent, 14 June 2024, esp. shared commitments and decision procedures; Ramaphosa, Opening of Parliament Address, 18 July 2024; Booysen (ed.), MISTRA report (Aug. 2024), especially coalition and early-test chapters, pp. 26–41 and 106–109; Schulz-Herzenberg and Southall (eds.), Election 2024 (Oct. 2024), publisher record; Chan, Journal of African Elections 24(1) (2025), pp. 125–126, DOI 10.20940/JAE/2025/v24i1a6; Presidency leaders’ statement, 3 Nov. 2025; Mashatile’s National Assembly oral replies, 11 Nov. 2025; 2026 State of the Nation Address; Presidency executive changes, 30 June 2026; Hill-Lewis, DA leader’s statement, 22 Sept. 2026.'
}));
