GEOGRAPHY_CASES.push(caseRecord({
  id: 'poland-2025-presidential-election-and-validation-dispute',
  labelId: 'poland-2025-presidential-election-and-validation-dispute',
  placeId: 'poland-election-2025',
  relationship: 'implemented',
  actor: 'National Electoral Commission (Państwowa Komisja Wyborcza); presidential candidates Karol Nawrocki and Rafał Trzaskowski and their committees; polling-station commissions; Supreme Court Chamber of Extraordinary Control and Public Affairs; Prosecutor General; election observers and challengers; and the National Assembly at the presidential oath',
  historicalSetting: 'The two rounds of Poland’s 2025 presidential election on 18 May and 1 June; the National Electoral Commission’s final result announcement; election protests and selected recounts; Supreme Court validation on 1 July; and the National Assembly oath on 6 August 2025.',
  startYear: 2025, endYear: 2025,
  periodLabel: '18 May–6 August 2025 · presidential election, review and assumption of office',
  datePrecision: 'year',
  evidenceKind: 'Polish election-authority, prosecutor, Supreme Court and parliamentary primary records; the OSCE/ODIHR limited election observation mission final report; European Court of Human Rights case law on the institutional standing of the validating chamber in a separate case; and two publisher-record/description-only book-length contexts',
  confidence: 'medium', reviewedAt: '2026-09-25',
  claim: `The Polish National Electoral Commission’s final announcement reports that, in the 1 June 2025 run-off, Karol Nawrocki received 10,606,877 votes (50.89 per cent) and Rafał Trzaskowski 10,237,286 (49.11 per cent). This is the official certified tally, not a claim about voter motives. The OSCE Office for Democratic Institutions and Human Rights (ODIHR) assessed the election in a limited observation mission: it described a competitive choice in a highly polarized environment, efficient election administration and concerns about transparency, media, campaign-finance and judicial independence. ODIHR expressly did not conduct systematic or comprehensive polling-station observation; its favorable account of observed polling and count procedures is therefore not a census or a nationwide recount. After challenges concerning protocol errors, the Supreme Court authorized recounts in 13 polling stations and inspection of eight more. ODIHR reports the Court received over 54,000 challenges, found 21 valid, and concluded none individually or collectively could alter the result; on 1 July the Chamber of Extraordinary Control and Public Affairs confirmed validity amid disputes over its independence and a process ODIHR described as lacking full transparency. Prosecutor General communications later reported a separate expert examination of ballots in 250 selected high-risk precincts, identifying discrepancies in 84; the corrected reported differences were 1,239 fewer Nawrocki votes and 1,242 more Trzaskowski votes than in the protocols. This model-selected subset is not a random national sample, and its figures cannot be extrapolated to all polling stations. These prosecutor-reported discrepancies are not equivalent to a judicial finding that the nationwide result was altered. The ECHR’s 2021 Dolińska-Ficek and Ozimek judgment found violations concerning appointments to the same chamber in that distinct case. It supplies relevant institutional context but did not rule on the 2025 election or determine the Polish-law validity of its result. The National Assembly’s official record establishes that Nawrocki took the oath on 6 August. The event shows constitutional and electoral institutions operating alongside unresolved public-trust and judicial-independence disputes; it is not a conclusion that either candidate’s supporters, all voters, or Poland as a whole share one political outlook. Sadurski’s pre-2025 account of constitutional breakdown and the Oxford Handbook of Polish Politics provide background context only; neither is evidence about the 2025 election itself. No ideological label or six-axis score is assigned.`,
  limitation: 'This case is limited to the 2025 presidential election and formal validation/transfer of office through 6 August. ODIHR used a limited mission and expressly lacked comprehensive polling-station coverage. Recounts and the prosecutor’s 250-precinct review were selective; the latter was risk-model-selected, not a representative national sample, and ongoing investigations may alter the record. Official tallies, observer judgments, prosecutorial allegations/findings and court decisions are different kinds of evidence. The ECHR judgment concerned the chamber’s composition in another case and is not a ruling on the 2025 election. This record does not settle the chamber’s domestic-law status, every protest, the effect of any irregularity on individual ballots, voter intentions or the election’s overall legitimacy. The two books listed are dated contextual works consulted through publisher records/description only; neither provides book-length coverage of this 2025 episode. Contemporary scholarship on the election and further independent legal analysis remain research gaps. No claim is made about all of Polish political history, Poland or Poles, and no ideological or six-axis score is inferred.',
  sourceIds: ['polandNecPresidentialResults2025', 'polandOdihrPresidentialElectionFinal2025', 'polandSupremeCourtValidation2025', 'polandProsecutorBallotReview2025', 'polandSejmPresidentialOath2025', 'polandEchrDoliskaFicek2021', 'polandSadurskiConstitutionalBreakdown2019', 'polandOxfordHandbookPolitics2025'],
  locator: 'PKW final run-off announcement (1 June 2025); ODIHR LEOM final report (20 October 2025), especially executive summary and post-election review; Prosecutor General’s corrected ballot-review account (updated 6 August 2025); Supreme Court resolution I NSW 9779/25 (1 July 2025); National Assembly oath record (6 August 2025); ECHR Dolińska-Ficek and Ozimek v. Poland judgment (8 November 2021), a separate case; OUP publisher records for dated contextual works.'
}));

