  claim: 'The Presidency’s official profile records Joseph Aoun’s assumption of office on 9 January 2025; a Presidency retrospective dates the parliamentary consultations designating Nawaf Salam to 13 January and the cabinet’s formation to 8 February. These executive sources establish official chronology and self-description, not independent evaluation of the new government. The Ministry of Interior’s 2025 results portal and UNDP’s nationwide key-results report document municipal and mukhtar elections; LADE’s monitoring with UNDP technical support separately reports women’s participation and results using candidate and voter surveys and observation. Those sources illuminate local electoral participation but do not establish equal access, uniform conditions or voter motivations.\n\nParliament’s account says 76 MPs voted on 9 March 2026 to extend the legislature’s mandate by two years; Law No. 41/2026 was published the same day. IPU Parline records the previously announced 10 May 2026 parliamentary election date and the extension through 31 May 2028. These records establish the formal decision and schedule, not public consent or the necessity and proportionality of postponement. On 7 April, Constitutional Council Decision 7/2026 unanimously rejected three petitions challenging the law. The Council reasoned from the exceptional security and military circumstances it described, while also stating that if those circumstances ended, Parliament should shorten the extended mandate and hold elections. The Council’s description of conditions and legal reasoning are attributed to the judgment; this entry does not independently verify the security situation.\n\nFor context rather than direct evidence of 2025–2026 events, Traboulsi’s modern history, Corm’s distinct French-language account, Salloukh and colleagues’ analysis of sectarianism, and work by Baumann, Cammett, and Karam and Majed offer independent approaches to Lebanon’s state formation, postwar institutions, political economy, welfare and protest. Their coverage predates this episode and their use here is contextual, not a claim that they analyzed the later events. No six-axis scores are assigned: the reviewed evidence concerns institutional chronology and electoral procedure, not a sufficiently comprehensive contemporary account of economic policy, social outcomes, authority in practice, identity, foreign relations and religion.',
  limitation: 'This is a bounded institutional and electoral episode, not a continuous history through September 2026, a verdict on the government or Constitutional Council, or a score for Lebanon, Lebanese people, any sect, electorate, candidate, party or locality. Official records establish offices, published results, legal enactment and judicial disposition; they do not by themselves establish electoral fairness, public consent, policy delivery or representative attitudes. The Presidency sources are interested executive accounts; municipal results and LADE monitoring have distinct methods and coverage; the Constitutional Council’s account of the security situation is not independent field verification. The cited book-length scholarship supplies multiple historical and institutional perspectives but predates this episode; no event-specific monograph or scholarly book covering the full 2025–April 2026 period was located. Arabic-language contemporary scholarship, independent security and election observation, implementation after the Council’s decision, and developments after April 2026 remain open research gaps. No protected text is reproduced.',
  sourceIds: ['lebanonPresidencyJosephAounProfile2025', 'lebanonPresidencyGovernmentFormation2025', 'lebanonMoimMunicipalElections2025', 'lebanonUndpMunicipalResults2025', 'lebanonLadeUndpGenderMonitoring2025', 'lebanonParliamentExtensionLaw2026', 'lebanonParliamentExtensionVote2026', 'lebanonConstitutionalCouncilExtension2026', 'lebanonConstitution1990Parliament', 'lebanonIpu2022Election', 'lebanonTraboulsiModernHistory2012', 'lebanonCormContemporary2012French', 'lebanonSalloukhSectarianism2015', 'lebanonBaumannCitizenHariri2016', 'lebanonCammettCompassionateCommunalism2014', 'lebanonKaramMajedUprising2022'],
