  confidence: 'medium',
  reviewedAt: '2026-09-25',
  claim: `This case begins at the 2024 uprising and does not duplicate the preceding 2014–2024 case. The UN human-rights office’s fact-finding report investigates violations and abuses related to the July–August protests; it documents an episode and its own findings, not a uniform account of every participant or the whole transition. The interim period reopened party competition and constitutional-reform debate. The 2025 July National Charter is evidence of negotiated proposals and recorded reservations, not proof of universal agreement or implementation. The EU Election Observation Mission’s 2026 report describes the 12 February election as competitive, credible and competently managed, while also identifying local political violence, weak enforcement of some campaign rules, limited political space for women, digital harms, media constraints and representation concerns affecting Indigenous and religious-minority groups. These are the mission’s attributed findings, not a country score. It records that women were four per cent of contestants and seven women were elected among the 297 declared constituency returns. TIB separately described the process as acceptably fair and competitive while reporting code violations and irregularities in its own 70-constituency sample; those sample findings cannot be generalized to every constituency. On the six axes, only bounded observations are warranted: economic distribution and social inclusion arose in the transition and campaign, but this source set does not establish policy implementation or public preference; authority is directly evidenced through the interim administration, reform process, election administration and return to an elected Parliament, but constitutional proposals and election observation do not prove durable accountability; identity debates are visible in charter negotiations but are not reducible to one national consensus; foreign orientation and religion are insufficiently documented here for broader interpretation, although observer reporting identifies specific minority-representation issues. No scores or whole-country ideological labels are assigned.`,
  limitation: 'A bounded 2024–2026 transition and election case, not an account of all Bangladesh since 2024, a verdict on the uprising or interim government, or a score for Bangladeshis, parties, students, women, Indigenous peoples, religious minorities, regions or voters. OHCHR findings concern its specified protest-period investigation; the EU mission’s assessments and TIB’s monitoring have distinct methods and institutional positions. TIB’s 70-constituency sample is not national prevalence. The Charter’s negotiated contents do not establish consensus or implementation. Official parliamentary and referendum result discrepancies and subsequent corrections require a separate primary-record audit; this case therefore makes no claim about referendum totals. The cited 2024 books provide independent historical context but predate the uprising. No published post-uprising book-length political analysis was available for full consultation at the review date; the full Charter implementation record, Bangla-language scholarship and testimony, constitutional litigation, local variation, minority and Indigenous political experience, platform implementation and developments after the election remain research gaps. No copyrighted wording or tables are reproduced.',
  sourceIds: ['bangladeshOhchrJulyAugust2024', 'bangladeshEeom2026FinalReport', 'bangladeshTibElectionTracking2026', 'bangladeshJulyNationalCharter2025Translation', 'bangladeshConstitution1972', 'riazPathwaysAutocratizationBangladesh2024', 'jahanSobhanFiftyYearsBangladesh2024', 'orRashidFiftyYearsBangladesh2024', 'hoqueChowdhuryConstitutionBangladesh2024'],
  locator: 'OHCHR Fact-Finding Report on the July and August 2024 protests, published 12 February 2025; July National Charter 2025 unofficial English translation, including the reform proposals and recorded reservations; EU Election Observation Mission, Bangladesh 2026 Final Report, especially executive summary, political context, participation of women and minorities, results and post-election environment; TIB, 13th National Election Process Tracking and Affidavits release (2026), including the stated constituency sample; Constitution of Bangladesh; Riaz, Pathways of Autocratization; Jahan and Sobhan (eds.), Fifty Years of Bangladesh; Or-Rashid, Understanding Fifty Years of Bangladesh Politics; Hoque and Chowdhury (eds.), A History of the Constitution of Bangladesh. The books are contextual sources, not event-specific accounts.',
