GEOGRAPHY_CASES.push(caseRecord({
  id: 'colombia-2026-electoral-transition-and-contested-results',
  labelId: 'colombia-2026-electoral-transition-and-contested-results',
  placeId: 'colombia',
  relationship: 'implemented',
  actor: 'The 2026 congressional and presidential candidates and campaigns; voters without presuming a common motive; the Registraduría and National Electoral Council (CNE); Congress; outgoing president Gustavo Petro and president-elect/incoming president Abelardo de la Espriella; the OAS, EU Election Observation Mission and Colombian civil-society observer MOE; and the Council of State',
  historicalSetting: 'Colombia’s 8 March 2026 congressional election, 31 May presidential first round, 21 June runoff, 24 June certification, 7 August inauguration, and election-litigation snapshot through 25 September 2026',
  startYear: 2026, endYear: 2026,
  periodLabel: '2026 · congressional and presidential elections, certified transfer of office, observer assessments and pending nullity proceeding',
  datePrecision: 'year',
  evidenceKind: 'official electoral portals and certification; Senate inauguration record; OAS, EU and Colombian civil-society election observation; official Council of State case notice; four book-length historical/contextual works predating the episode',
  confidence: 'medium', reviewedAt: '2026-09-25',
  claim: `This is a separate 2026 episode, not a continuation of the prior Colombia record beyond its 2022 endpoint. The Registraduría dates the congressional election to 8 March, and its presidential portal records the runoff; both explicitly distinguish the preliminary preconteo, which has no legal value, from the official escrutinios. The National Electoral Council’s Resolution E-3181 (24 June) certified Abelardo de la Espriella and José Manuel Restrepo elected for 2026–2030. The Senate records their swearing-in before Congress on 7 August in Cali. These primary records establish the official electoral and institutional sequence, not voter motives, equal conditions, public consensus or the quality of subsequent governing. The OAS’s 2 June report covers the 31 May first round: its 96 observers were present in 26 departments, Bogotá and five overseas cities. It characterized polling day as generally peaceful and orderly, while reporting campaign polarization, narratives undermining trust, harassment and stigmatization of journalists, and stakeholder concerns about intimidation and campaigning restrictions in some places. The OAS cautioned that studies did not conclusively establish how coercion affected particular political groups; campaign complaints and allegations are not findings of misconduct. Its direct poll-day observations covered 412 polling stations and 1,340 tables in its stated footprint, not every table. The EU mission’s 3 September final-report announcement characterized the elections as transparent, peaceful and well-organized, while recommending reforms concerning campaign finance and online political advertising, media pluralism, women’s congressional representation and voting access for more than 250,000 young people excluded by the voter-roll cutoff. These are the mission’s attributed assessments and recommendations, not proof of equal participation or policy outcomes. MOE Colombia is a separate domestic civil-society observation source; its second-round reporting is used as a complementary bounded observer account, not as a complete audit. On 24 August the Council of State admitted a nullity demand alleging psychological violence during the campaign and denied interim suspension. It explicitly said the preliminary decision did not resolve whether the alleged conduct occurred or affected voters or the result; further evidence and a final judgment remained outstanding in the official notice consulted. That procedural order neither invalidates nor finally upholds the election’s merits. Palacios, Bushnell, Richani and Gutiérrez-Sanín provide distinct pre-event book-length context on Colombian political history, conflict and clientelism, but none studies this election. The constitutional and election records make electoral authority and judicial contestation the best-evidenced dimension; observer recommendations identify bounded participation and inclusion questions. This material does not support a six-axis profile, causal account of voter choice or ideological classification of the winner, losing candidates, parties, electorate or Colombia. No numerical scores or related ideological labels are assigned.`,
  limitation: 'One country-level election episode is bounded to 2026 and the materials available through 25 September 2026; it does not bridge 2023–2025, describe subsequent policy or establish a current-country ideological claim. Official returns and inauguration records establish formal outcomes only. Observer missions have distinct mandates, dates, methods and coverage; the OAS sample is not a census, and reports of intimidation, campaign violations or political interference are attributed complaints/concerns unless expressly found by an authority. The EU public summary is a release of its final report, not a substitute for all underlying detail. The cited Council of State notice describes a preliminary procedural decision and no final merits judgment was located for that proceeding by the review date. The MOE material consulted is a bounded civil-society observer report. No event-specific book-length study was located; four book sources provide only older historical context, with publisher/catalog records rather than full texts consulted for this update. Spanish-language electoral scholarship, campaign platforms and implementation, detailed adjudication through final judgment, local variation, electorate motivations, and post-inauguration developments remain research gaps. No election tables or copyrighted report wording are republished.',
  sourceIds: ['colombiaRegistraduriaCongress2026', 'colombiaRegistraduriaPresidential2026', 'colombiaCnePresidentCertification2026', 'colombiaSenateInauguration2026', 'colombiaEuEomFinalReport2026', 'colombiaOasPresidentialFirstRoundObservation2026', 'colombiaMoePresidentialSecondRoundObservation2026', 'colombiaCouncilStateElectionChallengeAug2026', 'colombiaConstitution1991Presidency', 'colombiaPalaciosLegitimacyViolence2006', 'colombiaBushnellModern1993', 'colombiaRichaniSystemsViolence2013', 'colombiaGutierrezClientelisticWarfare2019'],
  locator: 'Registraduría Nacional del Estado Civil, 2026 congressional and presidential election portals (official escrutinios); Consejo Nacional Electoral, Resolution E-3181 (24 June 2026); Senate of the Republic, inauguration record (7 August); OAS Electoral Observation Mission, Second Report (2 June); EU Election Observation Mission, Final Report and 3 September release; MOE Colombia, second-round observation statements; Council of State, 24 August notice on nullity demand and interim suspension; Palacios (2006), Bushnell (1993), Richani (2013), and Gutiérrez-Sanín (2019) for earlier history/context only.',
}));

