GEOGRAPHY_CASES.push(caseRecord({
  id: 'tunisia-2024-presidential-election-and-electoral-contestation',
  labelId: 'tunisia-2024-presidential-election-and-electoral-contestation',
  placeId: 'tunisia',
  relationship: 'implemented',
  actor: 'The Independent High Authority for Elections (ISIE); the Administrative Court and Tunis Court of Appeal; the Assembly of the Representatives of the People; incumbent/presidential candidate Kaïs Saïed; challengers Ayachi Zammel and Zouhair Maghzaoui; other excluded or detained potential candidates and their advocates; election observers and rights organizations; and participating voters without presuming a shared motive',
  historicalSetting: 'Tunisia’s first presidential election under the 25 July 2022 Constitution, held on 6 October 2024, with pre-election candidacy and jurisdictional disputes and the final-results proclamation of 11 October',
  startYear: 2024, endYear: 2024,
  periodLabel: '2024 · presidential election, contested candidacy and jurisdiction, official final results',
  datePrecision: 'snapshot',
  evidenceKind: 'ISIE election portal and final decision; 2024 Organic Law No. 45; African Union preliminary observation; IFES legal-framework analysis; Amnesty International pre-election reporting; four book-length scholarly contexts published before the contest',
  confidence: 'medium', reviewedAt: '2026-09-25',
  claim: `This case adds a distinct 2024 election snapshot to the earlier Tunisian constitutional history, which ends in 2022. The ISIE’s French-language election portal records 6 October as polling day and reports a final participation rate of 28.79% from 9,753,217 registered voters; it displays Kaïs Saïed with 90.69%, Ayachi Zammel with 7.35% and Zouhair Maghzaoui with 1.97%. The agency’s Decision No. 552, dated 11 October and linked from its official Arabic site, declares the final results. These are the election authority’s official figures and act, not evidence of voters’ shared motives, an uncontested mandate or electoral quality. The election took place under the 2022 Constitution. The African Union’s 40-observer preliminary mission, present in 15 governorates, described a peaceful election day and effective poll administration, but expressly said its statement was preliminary rather than an overall or final process assessment. It also reported a tense legal-political context: the Administrative Court had ordered three candidates reinstated, ISIE did not implement the decisions, and Organic Law No. 45 of 28 September changed jurisdiction for presidential-election disputes from the Administrative Court to the Tunis Court of Appeal, including pending disputes. The law’s text and IFES’s contemporaneous legal-framework analysis establish the change; IFES assessed the late alteration as inconsistent with good electoral practice, a technical assessment rather than a court judgment. Amnesty International separately reported a wider pre-election crackdown and at least 97 arrests in its specified period and documentation; that rights finding is attributed to Amnesty, not a complete census or proof that every arrest was election-related. It must be read alongside the AU’s narrower observation window and its positive assessment of polling-day operations. On the six axes, authority is directly relevant through the official electoral process, the changed forum and unresolved questions about the relationship between court orders and election administration. Other dimensions lack sufficient case-specific evidence for an assessment. Sadiki and Saleh’s 2024 protest-history monograph and Hanau Santini’s 2018 study of post-revolutionary statehood offer distinct book-length contexts, alongside Gobe and Chouikha’s French-language political history and Zayani’s edited volume; all predate the election and none is treated as an explanation of its result. No ideological archetype, six-axis profile or numerical score is assigned to Tunisia, Saïed, other candidates, parties, voters or any community.`,
  limitation: 'This is a single 2024 election episode and not a continuous post-2022 account, a live political status report, or a score for Tunisia or any population, party, candidate, institution or voter. ISIE is the official electoral authority and its final decision establishes the declared result; neither its result page nor the decision independently establish fairness, voter motives or consensus. The AU statement is preliminary, based on a short-term 40-person mission deployed to 15 governorates and not a comprehensive final assessment; it reports positive polling-day administration as well as candidacy and jurisdiction concerns. IFES provides legal analysis, not election-day observation or a judicial ruling. Amnesty’s arrest figure is its attributed documentation over a stated reporting period and should not be converted into election-specific incidence or national prevalence. The exact underlying Administrative Court judgments and a comprehensive merits-level audit were not independently assembled for this update. The books cited are pre-election contexts documented via publisher records; their full texts were not newly consulted, and their publication metadata cannot substantiate 2024 events. Arabic and Tunisian scholarly interpretations, fuller local observation, detailed case-by-case court records, candidate platforms, socioeconomic voting evidence and developments after October 2024 remain open research gaps. No copyrighted report or legal text is reproduced.',
  sourceIds: ['tunisiaIsiePresidentialPortal2024', 'tunisiaIsieFinalDecision5522024', 'tunisiaElectionLawAmendment45', 'tunisiaAuObservation2024', 'tunisiaIfesElectionAssessment2024', 'tunisiaAmnestyPreElectionCrackdown2024', 'tunisiaSantiniLimitedStatehood2018', 'tunisiaSadikiSalehProtestscapes2024', 'tunisiaConstitution2022', 'zayaniFledglingDemocracyTunisia2023', 'gobeChouikhaTunisia2015'],
  locator: 'ISIE, Presidential Election 2024 results portal (French, with Arabic official report link); ISIE Decision No. 552 declaring final results (11 October 2024, Arabic); Organic Law No. 2024-45 (28 September 2024, Arabic/French legal database witnesses); African Union Election Observation Mission preliminary statement (8 October); IFES, Elections in Tunisia: Frequently Asked Questions (September 2024); Amnesty International pre-election briefing (September 2024); Hanau Santini (2018), Sadiki and Saleh (2024), Gobe and Chouikha (2015), and Zayani (ed., 2023) for dated context only.',
}));

GEOGRAPHY_LABELS.push({ id: 'sudanese-wartime-authority-and-civilian-protection-crisis', name: 'Sudanese wartime authority and civilian-protection crisis', aliases: ['Sudan war 2023–2025', 'Sudan SAF–RSF war', 'Sudan Jeddah ceasefire and civilian protection'], family: 'Postcolonial conflict, fragmented military authority, revolution and civilian-protection politics', description: 'A bounded political-history case on Sudan’s war beginning 15 April 2023, the formal SAF–RSF Jeddah commitments and the documented civilian-protection crisis through the first half of 2025. It distinguishes armed-party claims, formal agreements, UN-documented violations and Sudanese civilian political agency; it is not a national ideological profile or a claim that either armed party represents Sudanese society.', sourceIds: ['sudanJeddahShortCeasefire2023', 'sudanOhchrHumanRightsJanJun2025', 'casciarriFranckBakhitRevolutionContinues2026', 'berridgeUnfinishedDemocracy2022', 'deWaalRealPoliticsHorn2015', 'oetteBabikerConstitutionMaking2019'], relatedEntries: [] });

GEOGRAPHY_CASES.push(caseRecord({
  id: 'sudan-2023-25-war-fragmented-authority-and-civilian-protection',
  labelId: 'sudanese-wartime-authority-and-civilian-protection-crisis',
  placeId: 'sudan',
  relationship: 'experimented',
  actor: 'Sudanese Armed Forces (SAF) and Rapid Support Forces (RSF), their commanders and aligned forces; Sudanese civilians, local resistance committees and emergency-response volunteers; displaced communities; and the Saudi–US Jeddah facilitators. These actors are not treated as collectively representative of Sudan.',
  historicalSetting: 'The war that began in Khartoum on 15 April 2023, the SAF–RSF Jeddah ceasefire and humanitarian-arrangements agreement of 20 May 2023, and the conflict and civilian-protection reporting horizon from January through June 2025.',
  startYear: 2023, endYear: 2025,
  periodLabel: '2023–2025 · SAF–RSF war, failed/contested ceasefire commitments, fragmented authority and civilian protection through June 2025',
  datePrecision: 'approximate',
  evidenceKind: 'Primary ceasefire agreement, OHCHR country reporting for a defined period, and a 2026 scholarly edited-volume record; earlier book-length works provide pre-war transitional and regional context only',
  confidence: 'medium',
  reviewedAt: '2026-09-25',
  claim: 'The Saudi Ministry of Foreign Affairs’ Arabic text records that SAF and RSF representatives signed a short-term ceasefire and humanitarian-arrangements agreement in Jeddah on 20 May 2023 with Saudi and US facilitation. That is evidence of formal commitments, not proof of compliance or an implemented settlement. OHCHR’s report covering 1 January–30 June 2025 documents at least 3,384 civilian deaths in the context of conflict during that period, says the real toll was likely significantly higher, and records killings outside hostilities, attacks on civilian infrastructure, sexual violence, arbitrary detention and restrictions or attacks on civil-society figures. These are the UN office’s documented findings and attribution, not a complete census or court judgment. The 2026 edited volume’s publisher record identifies original fieldwork and a Willow Berridge chapter tracing three post-uprising transitions between centre, periphery, revolution and war; it supplies a recent scholarly frame, but the full book was not consulted here. Berridge, de Waal and Oette–Babiker offer distinct pre-war accounts of the revolution, political marketplace and constitutional questions; they contextualize the transition, not the 2023–2025 battlefield or every Sudanese political experience. These materials support a bounded account of armed authority, conflict commitments, civilian harm and civic survival; they do not support six-axis scores, a single ideology for Sudan, or any claim that SAF, RSF, a civilian coalition or a locality speaks for all Sudanese.',
  limitation: 'This case is bounded to the war beginning 15 April 2023 and OHCHR’s specified 1 January–30 June 2025 documentation horizon; it is not a present-day control map or a continuous account through 2026. Jeddah is a text of commitments by two armed parties, not a finding of compliance, public consent or comprehensive peace. OHCHR findings are limited to its documentation, stated period and methods; the casualty number is a minimum, not a comprehensive count, and reported allegations must not be converted into individual criminal findings. The 2026 scholarly source was consulted through publisher metadata and chapter descriptions, not the full text; it is a book-length interpretation of the revolution/war transition, not independent verification of each event. Berridge–de Waal–Lynch–Makawi (2022), de Waal (2015) and Oette–Babiker (2019) predate the war and are used for context only. Arabic-language scholarship and primary texts, local histories outside Khartoum and Darfur, women’s and displaced people’s testimony, regional armed and civilian actors, humanitarian access, command responsibility, the full Jeddah monitoring record, later ceasefire/diplomatic initiatives and post-June-2025 developments remain open research tasks. This is not a score for Sudanese people, ethnic, regional, linguistic or religious communities, either armed party, civic actors or any government; the gap between the earlier 1989–2021 Sudan case and this 2023–2025 snapshot remains explicit.',
  sourceIds: ['sudanJeddahShortCeasefire2023', 'sudanOhchrHumanRightsJanJun2025', 'casciarriFranckBakhitRevolutionContinues2026', 'berridgeUnfinishedDemocracy2022', 'deWaalRealPoliticsHorn2015', 'oetteBabikerConstitutionMaking2019'],
  locator: 'Saudi Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Jeddah short-term ceasefire and humanitarian-arrangements agreement (Arabic, 20 May 2023); OHCHR, Human rights situation in the Sudan, 1 January to 30 June 2025 (published 19 September 2025); Casciarri, Franck and Bakhit (eds.), The Revolution Continues (2026), especially publisher-listed chapter 2 by Willow Berridge, pp. 35–54; older book-length sources are pre-war contexts only.',
}));

GEOGRAPHY_LABELS.push({ id: 'cote-divoire-2025-presidential-election-and-candidacy-contestation', name: 'Côte d’Ivoire 2025 presidential election and candidacy contestation', aliases: ['Ivorian presidential election 2025', 'Côte d’Ivoire October 2025 election', 'Ouattara 2025 election', 'Ivorian candidate eligibility and civic space 2025'], family: 'Postcolonial constitutional, electoral and civic-space politics', description: 'A bounded account of the 25 October 2025 presidential election, the definitive five-candidate list, the official result and the surrounding contestation over candidate eligibility and peaceful assembly. The official judicial record, limited preliminary observation and attributed rights reporting are kept distinct; this is an election-specific research label, not an ideological identity or assessment of Côte d’Ivoire as a whole.', sourceIds: ['coteDivCeiPresidentialResults2025', 'coteDivConstitutionalCouncilCandidates2025', 'coteDivConstitutionalCouncilFinalResults2025', 'coteDivEcowasAuPreliminaryObservation2025', 'coteDivAmnestyPreElectionProtests2025', 'coteDivAmnestyAnnualReport2025', 'kofiAnnanElectoralVulnerabilityCoteDiv2025', 'marzinCoteDivElectionHistory1945to2025'], relatedEntries: [] });

GEOGRAPHY_CASES.push(caseRecord({
  id: 'cote-divoire-2025-presidential-election-candidacy-and-civic-space',
  labelId: 'cote-divoire-2025-presidential-election-and-candidacy-contestation',
  placeId: 'cote-divoire',
  relationship: 'implemented',
  actor: 'The Independent Electoral Commission (CEI) and Constitutional Council; Alassane Ouattara and the four other officially listed candidates Simone Ehivet Gbagbo, Henriette Lagou, Jean-Louis Billon and Ahoua Don-Mello; PPA-CI and PDCI-RDA; opposition supporters and peaceful-assembly participants; Ivorian security and administrative authorities; and the joint ECOWAS–African Union observation mission. No actor or party is treated as representing all Ivorian voters.',
  historicalSetting: 'Côte d’Ivoire’s 25 October 2025 presidential election, the Constitutional Council’s definitive candidate-list decision of 8 September, the pre-election assembly restrictions and protests, the CEI count and Constitutional Council’s final proclamation of 4 November, and Amnesty’s retrospective reporting on the 2025 electoral period.',
  startYear: 2025, endYear: 2025,
  periodLabel: '2025 · presidential election, candidate eligibility, observed polling, official result and civic-space contestation',
  datePrecision: 'snapshot',
  evidenceKind: 'French-language official electoral and Constitutional Council records, joint ECOWAS–AU preliminary observation, Amnesty International rights reporting in English/French, a pre-election institutional risk assessment and book-length historical contexts',
  confidence: 'medium',
  reviewedAt: '2026-09-25',
  claim: 'The Constitutional Council’s 8 September definitive decision listed five candidates: Alassane Ouattara, Simone Ehivet Gbagbo, Henriette Lagou, Jean-Louis Billon and Ahoua Don-Mello. It also declared inadmissible for lack of standing the PPA-CI and PDCI-RDA petitions challenging Ouattara’s eligibility; that procedural ruling concerns those petitions and does not resolve every dispute about the candidacy list or the political fairness of the rules. Amnesty’s contemporaneous account separately reported that the Council had invalidated the candidacies of Laurent Gbagbo and Tidjane Thiam, two prominent opposition leaders; these are Amnesty’s attributed descriptions, not an inference that the September ruling on Ouattara decided their eligibility. The CEI national table and Council’s 4 November final decision record 4,292,474 voters from 8,568,456 registered (50.10%) and Ouattara’s 3,759,030 votes (89.77%). The Council states that no complaints about regularity or counting were registered during the statutory window, finds no irregularity affecting the overall result and proclaims Ouattara elected in the first round; those are the Council’s official determinations, not a project audit of every polling station or evidence of common voter motives. The joint ECOWAS–AU mission’s preliminary report describes the opening as peaceful and orderly at the 88 polling stations it observed, but records that 32.20% of that limited opening sample experienced delays, often linked to late materials or equipment; this preliminary sample is not a final nationwide audit. Amnesty’s 16 October briefing documents its account of the 11 October protest dispersal in Abidjan and reports authorities’ figure of 255 arrests in Abidjan and Dabou that day. Its retrospective 2025 country entry attributes a broader total of 1,658 arrests and 11 deaths since 11 October to a National Security Council statement; those figures are not converted here into a complete protest-related casualty census. Thus the formal election result, the observers’ bounded operational findings, and restrictions on civic assembly are distinct strands: neither the high official vote share nor the Council’s no-complaint statement establishes unopposed social consensus, while the rights reports do not by themselves adjudicate the result. Marzin’s 2025 312-page French-language electoral history spans 1945–2025, but only its publisher metadata was consulted. McGovern’s local ethnography of war-making, Bouquet’s French-language geopolitical study and Koffi’s earlier political history are pre-election contexts, not direct evidence about the 2025 vote. The episode does not support a six-axis profile or an ideological classification of Côte d’Ivoire, Ouattara, a party, excluded candidates or the electorate.',
  limitation: 'This is one presidential-election episode, bounded to 2025 rather than a continuous national history or current-government assessment. The Constitutional Council’s definitive candidate list and final-results decision establish official legal acts and its stated findings; they do not constitute independent judgment on all candidacy disputes, ballot access, campaign conditions, turnout validity, local count quality or public consent. The ECOWAS–AU statement is preliminary and its reported opening observations cover only 88 stations, not all polling locations or the full process. Amnesty’s event accounts and annual country entry have distinct methods and reporting windows; the broader arrests/deaths figures are attributed to the National Security Council and are not assumed to be exclusively protest-caused. The Kofi Annan Foundation/Electoral Integrity Project assessment predates the poll and is used only as a risk snapshot. Marzin’s 312-page book is event-spanning but was consulted through publisher metadata only, not read; no full event-specific scholarly monograph was directly assessed. McGovern, Bouquet and Koffi cover earlier periods and provide only distinct background perspectives, not verification of 2025 events. French- and Nouchi-language local scholarship, full party platforms and campaign-finance records, polling-station-level observer evidence, the underlying protest/detention court files, participant interviews, the government’s detailed responses, and scholarship on the post-2025 presidential term remain open. This is not a score for Côte d’Ivoire or any Ivorian community, party, candidate, voter, institution or authority, and the existing 1960–2020 case is not silently extended across 2021–2024.',
  sourceIds: ['coteDivCeiPresidentialResults2025', 'coteDivConstitutionalCouncilCandidates2025', 'coteDivConstitutionalCouncilFinalResults2025', 'coteDivEcowasAuPreliminaryObservation2025', 'coteDivAmnestyPreElectionProtests2025', 'coteDivAmnestyAnnualReport2025', 'kofiAnnanElectoralVulnerabilityCoteDiv2025', 'marzinCoteDivElectionHistory1945to2025', 'coteDivPresidenceConstitution2016', 'mcgovernMakingWarCoteIvoire2011', 'bouquetGeopolitiqueCoteIvoire2008', 'koffiHouphouetMutations2010'],
  locator: 'CEI, Élection du Président de la République, scrutin du 25 octobre 2025, résultats nationaux; Conseil constitutionnel, Décisions CI-2025-EP-006 (8 September) and CI-2025-EP-007 (4 November); ECOWAS–AU Preliminary Declaration (27 October; opening observations, §§18–21); Amnesty International, pre-election briefing (16 October) and Côte d’Ivoire 2025 annual country entry; Kofi Annan Foundation/Electoral Integrity Project pre-election analysis (June 2025); Marzin publisher record and pre-2021 McGovern, Bouquet and Koffi book records for context only.',
}));

