GEOGRAPHY_CASES.push(caseRecord({
  id: 'haitian-2026-post-council-electoral-transition',
  labelId: 'haitian-2026-post-council-electoral-transition',
  placeId: 'haiti',
  relationship: 'implemented',
  actor: 'The Transitional Presidential Council and its signatories; Prime Minister Alix Didier Fils-Aimé and the Council of Ministers; the Provisional Electoral Council (CEP); political parties and coalitions that signed or criticized the post-Council pact; BINUH and the UN Security Council. These institutions and named political coalitions do not represent all Haitians or all organized political forces.',
  historicalSetting: 'The Transitional Presidential Council’s mandate under the 2024 political arrangement ended on 7 February 2026 without the scheduled installation of an elected president. Executive functions passed to the Council of Ministers led by the Prime Minister. A National Pact for Stability and the Organization of Elections was signed on 21 February and presented publicly on 23 February. The CEP’s 23 December 2025 calendar had set first-round elections for 30 August 2026; its 27 July 2026 revised calendar moved the first round and proposed constitutional ratification to 13 December, with second-round and local elections scheduled for 21 February 2027. As of the CEP’s 21 September update, voter registration remained underway.',
  startYear: 2026, endYear: 2026,
  periodLabel: '2026 snapshot · post-Council executive arrangement, political pact and revised conditional election timetable',
  datePrecision: 'snapshot',
  evidenceKind: 'Haitian executive and electoral-authority primary records, signed political agreement, United Nations Secretary-General reporting, and distinct book-length political-economy contexts',
  confidence: 'medium',
  reviewedAt: '2026-09-25',
  claim: `The United Nations Secretary-General reported that the Transitional Presidential Council’s mandate ended on 7 February 2026 and executive authority was immediately transferred to the Council of Ministers under Prime Minister Alix Didier Fils-Aimé. The Council of Ministers subsequently asserted full executive authority under article 149 of the amended 1987 Constitution and article 28.1 of the 27 May 2024 decree. The Prime Minister’s own address described the handover as continuity of the State; this is the Government’s constitutional account, not an independent judicial determination. The planned handover to elected authorities by 7 February 2026 did not occur, and Haiti had no elected president installed by that date.

The post-Council National Pact for Stability and the Organization of Elections, signed on 21 February and presented by the Government on 23 February, records an agreement among participating political, economic and civil-society actors to organize elections and govern during the interim. The UN describes a broad cross-section of signatories, while also recording political groups’ calls for more inclusive dialogue and opposition coalitions’ criticism of the authorities’ inability to convene elections and demands for different transition arrangements. A negotiated pact is evidence of its signatories’ commitments, not of universal Haitian agreement or constitutional adjudication.

Electoral timetables are plans, not completed votes. The CEP’s December 2025 calendar scheduled the first presidential and legislative round for 30 August 2026, conditional on acceptable security and sufficient financing. On 27 July 2026, after the Prime Minister warned that prevailing security conditions might prevent August voting, the CEP issued a replacement calendar setting first-round presidential and legislative elections and proposed constitutional ratification for 13 December 2026, with second-round and local elections set for 21 February 2027. The revised calendar likewise conditions adherence on security and funding. The CEP’s 21 September update reported voter registration still underway, 1,285 operational registration/voting centres and 628,755 registered voters; these administrative figures are the electoral authority’s report, not an independent audit of access, coverage or voter participation. No election or constitutional ratification had taken place by the review date.

The transition is a contest over institutional authority, constitutional procedure, electoral inclusion and security, but these facts do not resolve the legal or popular legitimacy of the interim arrangement. Alex Dupuy’s English-language political-economy monograph (2024) connects the crisis through class, power, gender and foreign intervention; Eland Guerrier’s French-language political-crisis book (2025) offers a separate local legal-political perspective, with his disclosed party-advisory roles relevant to standpoint. Only their publisher records/descriptions were consulted, not either full text. The International Crisis Group’s 2025 interview-based report is a further, explicitly pre-February-2026 perspective. None supplies proof of the handover or later election preparations. Constitutional comparison is relevant but no ideology or six-axis score is assigned; the materials do not support a complete account of public opinion, political practice or the conditions faced across the country.`,
  limitation: `This is one institutional/electoral snapshot, bounded to the end of the Council mandate through the CEP’s 21 September 2026 progress update; it is not a continuous history of the 2021–2026 crisis or a claim about every political constituency, locality or resident. The February handover’s legal basis is presented through executive and UN accounts, not a court judgment; the Pact’s signatories and critics are not a representative sample. UN reports record attributed positions and mediation, not a national opinion poll. CEP dates and operational statistics are primary administrative claims, not proof that security, finances, access, inclusion or election readiness are adequate. The December 2026/February 2027 votes and constitutional ratification remain future, conditional plans, and the 21 September registration counts were not independently audited. Dupuy and Guerrier are distinct book-length contextual perspectives, but only publisher descriptions/metadata were consulted; Guerrier’s disclosed political roles matter to standpoint. Crisis Group’s 232 interviews ended in February 2025 and cannot establish the later transfer. The complete underlying decrees, official-gazette copy of the Pact, Haitian court interpretation, independent field verification and local testimony across departments, rural/urban communities, women, displaced people, political groupings and diaspora remain research needs. No Haitian or national consensus is inferred, no source prose or protected book content is reproduced, and no six-axis profile is assigned.`,
  sourceIds: ['haitiGovCouncilMandateEndFeb2026', 'haitiUnReportTransitionCouncilEnd2026', 'haitiUnReportElectoralTransitionJuly2026', 'haitiNationalPactStabilityElections2026', 'haitiCepCalendarAug2026Superseded', 'haitiCepRevisedCalendarDec2026', 'haitiCepRegistrationUpdateSep2026', 'haitiDupuySince1804CriticalPerspectives2024', 'haitiGuerrierPoliticalCrisis2025', 'haitiCrisisGroupLockedTransition2025'],
  locator: 'Government of Haiti address (7 February 2026); United Nations Secretary-General reports S/2026/325 (14 April) and S/2026/574 (14 July); National Pact (signed 21 February; presented 23 February); CEP original calendar (23 December 2025), revised calendar (CEP/DC/028-2526, 27 July 2026), and progress update (21 September 2026); Dupuy (2024) and Guerrier (2025) publisher records; International Crisis Group Report No. 107 (19 February 2025).',
}));

