  claim: 'UNSMIL records that a Smaller Convening agreement was signed in Tripoli on 30 August 2026 to address the first two milestones of its roadmap: reconstituting the Board of Commissioners of the High National Elections Commission (HNEC) and resolving outstanding constitutional and legal issues for presidential and legislative elections. UNSMIL says the agreement provides for adjustments to the electoral framework, including elections under a single executive authority, and commits its parties to work toward elections within no more than 24 months. These are negotiated provisions and a goal, not proof that a unified executive or revised legal framework had been established or that an election date had been fixed. The Security Council welcomed the agreement and urged support and implementation. UNSMIL reported House of Representatives endorsement following its 14 September session and an earlier statement by 103 members; this does not mean all members were present or that the separate High Council of State endorsed it. At a 20 September follow-up, participants continued to seek wider local-stakeholder support and scheduled another meeting for early October, showing an ongoing process rather than completed implementation. Lacher’s study of conflict fragmentation and Pargeter’s account of tribes and state provide distinct, earlier political-historical contexts, but neither is evidence about the 2026 agreement itself.',
